{
  "id": "chemist-sick",
  "uuid": "000df032-c061-5309-9149-c1e594db5eb5",
  "title": "Feeling Sick: At the Chemist",
  "category": "health",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "level": 6,
  "order": 602,
  "theme": "health",
  "culturalNote": "For everyday illnesses, Kenyans go to the chemist (pharmacy) first — the pharmacist asks about your symptoms, and you walk out with medicine and clear instructions. \"Pole!\" (sorry) is the first thing anyone says when you're unwell — it expresses sympathy, not an apology. Body-part nouns are a lovely place to see the Kenyan n/n habit: \"kichwa inauma\" (my head hurts) is everyday speech, while Sanifu says \"kichwa kinauma\" because kichwa is ki/vi.",
  "startingPoint": "You've felt awful since yesterday. You walk into a chemist near your flat.",
  "grammarFocus": [
    "past-li",
    "negative-past",
    "object-infixes"
  ],
  "grammar": [
    "past-li",
